Every $\Delta$ is taken against the adult placed by the same rule, because the adult's own $D$ depends on the placement. It is << F.g3b_adult_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at its measured head position, << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at top contact, << F.g3b_adult_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB laterally centered and raised to contact and << F.g3b_adult_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB at true contact. In its own helmets scaled with the head it is << F.g3b_adult_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> and << F.g3b_adult_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB (centered and laterally centered; scale factor << F.g3b_adult_cfx_k >>), and in the helmet fitted at its own gap << F.rev_cgap_adult_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>~dB. $\Delta$ is reported descriptively, without a test or a margin; where a difference of two pooled medians replaces it, the text says so.

Peak-channel SNR divides each channel by its own noise, so magnetometers and gradiometers compete on equal terms. It is the closest analogue of the sensor-level SNRs of the clinical studies, except that a clinical baseline standard deviation also holds physiological noise, whereas the model's holds only what the noise model contains. Mean-power SNR is the channel-averaged SNR of \citet{goldenholz2009}, also used by \citet{matsubara2026}.

The plug-in filter is built from a Ledoit--Wolf covariance estimate \citep{ledoit2004} and evaluated under the true covariance, so it cannot exceed the oracle value. The estimate from << F.g2_plugin60s_samples >> samples is the declared << F.wr_cov_seconds_long >>-s scenario; its samples are independent, which does not establish the effective sample size of a measured colored-noise baseline. The estimate from << F.g2_plugin10s_samples >> samples shows how a short baseline penalizes the array with more channels.

Neighboring cortical targets are correlated, so no interval resamples targets as if they were independent, except in the adult's frequency-band comparison, whose intervals are therefore narrower than those of the primary comparison. All intervals are percentile intervals; in the spike study the location is the statistical unit (Section~\ref{sec:S7}). Table~\ref{tab:S16} gives the resample count of every interval family, and Table~\ref{tab:S2} lists every estimand of the study, its definition, its interval and what that interval covers.

